[
    {
        "content": "<p>Let E be a finite-dimensional real vector space. </p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:E\\to GL_{\\mathbb{R}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3322em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">R</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> be a function which is discontinuous everywhere.</p>\n<p>I now want to give <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> a charted space structure in the following way: for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e\\in E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> I identify the open set <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> of my space-to-be-charted <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> with the open set <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> of my model space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> using the linear map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(e)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>. So these identifications are jumping around in a crazy way.</p>\n<p>I now want to say that this charted space structure makes <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> into a differentiable manifold, because all transition maps are just the composite of two invertible linear maps so they're <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^\\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">∞</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>Have I made a mathematical error here? I haven't thought properly about the definition of a manifold for nearly 40 years. </p>\n<p>I am trying to supervise a student who is putting a Riemannian manifold structure on the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-sphere in an <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>-dimensional real inner product space, and to do this I'm trying to understand how we have made the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-sphere into a charted space. We seem to be using <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=stereographic%27#doc\">docs#stereographic'</a> (in the root namespace?) which seems to me to say \"given a point <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span></span></span></span> in the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-sphere, project away from <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span></span></span></span> onto the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-dimensional space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{v\\}^\\perp</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0991em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">}</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mrel mtight\">⊥</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>; this gives a homeomorphism between <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span></span></span></span> and a random <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-dimensional inner product space which varies with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span></span></span></span>; now use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=OrthonormalBasis.fromOrthogonalSpanSingleton#doc\">docs#OrthonormalBasis.fromOrthogonalSpanSingleton</a> to identify this random space with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>; this uses the fact that a finite-dimensional inner product space has an orthonormal basis, which is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=stdOrthonormalBasis#doc\">docs#stdOrthonormalBasis</a> , which uses the axiom of choice. So what I'm suggesting above must presumably be true, and somehow I just was not expecting this at all!</p>",
        "id": 612128959,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784725417
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Is.20this.20a.20manifold.3F/near/612128959\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I now want to say that this charted space structure makes <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> into a differentiable manifold, because all transition maps are just the composite of two invertible linear maps so they're <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^\\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">∞</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">.</span></span></span></span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, that's a perfect definition, both from the mathematical and the Lean point of view.</p>",
        "id": 612129509,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784725536
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Wow, thanks! I suspect that we are very lucky that whoever did this chose to make the identification in an inner-product-preserving way, otherwise making the sphere into a Riemannian manifold would be far harder than expected.</p>",
        "id": 612129753,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784725607
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think that was <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"260507\">Heather Macbeth</span> IIRC</p>",
        "id": 612223297,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1784752273
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/pull/5607\">mathlib3#5607</a></p>",
        "id": 612224084,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1784752595
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why do you need charts to make the sphere into a Riemannian manifold?</p>",
        "id": 612229785,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1784754994
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am trying to understand manifolds in Lean properly so I can teach them to a student. I don't know of any way to learn how manifolds work in Lean other than by ploughing through the code and working things out for myself. Last week I understood this ModelWithCorners thing and got as far as the definition of the tangent space of a point; this week in the meeting I understood how we created the tangent space of the sphere at a point under this convention that all tangent spaces should be equal to the model space despite the fact that in reality the tangent space of the n-sphere is a \"moving\" n-space. I am still not great on tangent bundles but there's always next week. One big breakthrough this week was that the variable named <code>EB</code> means \"model space of the Base\" not \"model space of the Bundle\". There is a lot of implicit knowledge here that users of this part of the library will know back to front but I don't know it and so I'm just reading the code and slowly working everything out from first principles.</p>",
        "id": 612233008,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784756626
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe one could use this opportunity to add a chapter on manifolds to MIL?</p>",
        "id": 612324419,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1784803081
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think there should be a chapter on manifolds in MIL (and one in the Lean phrasebook also)! Writing on eis on my medium-term TODO list.</p>",
        "id": 612333154,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784806455
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110038\">@Kevin Buzzard</span> I'm very happy to take a list of things that tripped you up and include them there. Are you aware of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib-phrasebook/pull/14\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib-phrasebook/pull/14</a> already?</p>",
        "id": 612333265,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784806497
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's a question I don't know how to answer. Is the tangent bundle of a manifold itself a manifold in Lean? The reason it's hard to just figure it out with <code>#synth</code> is that I cannot guess the model space/model with corners.</p>",
        "id": 612333994,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784806783
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The answer is yes: the total space of a C^n vector bundle is a C^n manifold, and the tangent bundle of a C^{n+1} manifold is a C^n manifold. Let me loogle for the declarations...</p>",
        "id": 612334229,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784806871
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=TangentBundle.contMDiffVectorBundle#doc\">docs#TangentBundle.contMDiffVectorBundle</a> is the second half, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bundle.TotalSpace.isManifold#doc\">docs#Bundle.TotalSpace.isManifold</a> the first half.</p>",
        "id": 612334449,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784806941
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In general, <code>I.tangent</code> should be the model you want (where <code>I</code> is the model with corners on the base manifold).</p>",
        "id": 612334498,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784806962
    }
]